= Adzuki Bean (赤小豆 / 小豆)

_Chinese: fully classical — 赤小豆 (chìxiǎodòu) is treated in the_ Shennong Bencao Jing _and in the_ Shanghan Lun _formulary, as the canonical pulse for damp-heat resolution and edema. Japanese: fully traditional — 小豆 (azuki) is a culturally foundational bean in Japan, used in_ sekihan _("red rice," ceremonial), in_ anko (red-bean paste, _the basis of nearly all traditional Japanese confectionery), and in folk medicine. Ayurveda: not classical — the species is East Asian and was not in the South Asian saṃhitā canon. Greco-Roman: not classical — adzuki is exclusively East Asian._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Vigna angularis_ (Willd.) Ohwi & H. Ohashi, family Fabaceae; older synonym _Phaseolus angularis_ Willd. East Asian origin (probably domesticated in central China c. 2000 BCE; widely cultivated in China, Japan, Korea). The drug-foodstuff is the *seed*. The genus _Vigna_ is the same as the mung bean (_V. radiata_) — adzuki and mung are closely-related species — see _mung-dhal.typ_.

*English:* _Adzuki_ (also _azuki_, _aduki_), direct transliteration of Japanese 小豆 (azuki). The Japanese reading 小豆 (azuki) is the standard global English term; the Mandarin reading is _xiǎodòu_ ("small bean"), the Chinese drug name is 赤小豆 (chìxiǎodòu, "red small bean").#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "adzuki, n."); the Japanese reading dominates global English usage.]

*Chinese:* 赤小豆 (chìxiǎodòu, "red small bean") — the canonical drug name in the Bencao tradition; 紅豆 (hóngdòu, "red bean") is sometimes used loosely for adzuki but also for other red beans (notably _Phaseolus vulgaris_ red kidney beans), creating ambiguity in modern Chinese cooking. The drug 赤小豆 is unambiguously _V. angularis_.#footnote[Character glosses: _Hanyu Da Cidian_; the 赤小豆 / 紅豆 distinction is real and a frequent translation issue.]

*Japanese:* 小豆 (azuki), "small bean." Read _azuki_ in the bean context (despite the 訓読み _kun'yomi_ pattern), _shōzu_ in the formal medical reading. The character pair preserves the Chinese "small bean" naming.

*Sanskrit:* No classical entry. Modern Hindi: not standardized; descriptive _japani lal lobiya_ (Japanese red cowpea) etc.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

赤小豆 is a fully canonical drug, in the _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (middle class, 中品) and treated in major formulas of the _Shanghan Lun_ tradition; it is the canonical *damp-heat-and-edema* pulse.#footnote[赤小豆 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_; _Bencao Gangmu_ entry; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; formula appearances: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

*Property (性):* Neutral (平), slightly cool. *Flavor (味):* Sweet (甘), slightly sour (酸). *Channels:* Heart (心), Small Intestine (小腸), Spleen (脾) [some sources add Kidney].

*Actions & indications:*
- 利水消腫 — promotes urination, *resolves edema* — the cardinal action; for water retention with damp-heat, ascites, pregnancy edema.
- 利濕退黃 — resolves damp-heat, alleviates jaundice — the basis of 麻黃連翹赤小豆湯 (Mahuang Lianqiao Chixiaodou Tang) of the _Shanghan Lun_, for damp-heat jaundice.
- 清熱解毒消癰 — clears heat, resolves toxicity, disperses abscess; topical paste and internal use for skin sores, mastitis.
- 養血 / 健脾 — modest blood-supportive and Spleen-strengthening (a less central action, supporting the daily-dietetic use).

*Cautions:* unsuitable for vacuity-cold patterns with frequent urination and yin-vacuity dryness — the diuretic action depletes fluids; long high-dose use can deplete _yin_.#footnote[Action list: _Bencao Gangmu_ 赤小豆 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; 麻黃連翹赤小豆湯 from _Shanghan Lun_.]

== Japanese cultural and folk-medical tradition

Beyond the formal CCM treatment, adzuki has *substantial cultural-medicinal standing in Japan*:

- *Sekihan (赤飯, "red rice")* — short-grain glutinous rice steamed with adzuki, producing a pink-tinged rice traditionally served at celebrations (weddings, birthdays, festivals); the red color is auspicious in East Asian symbolism.
- *Anko (餡子, red-bean paste)* — sweetened adzuki paste (varieties: _tsubu-an_ chunky, _koshi-an_ smooth, _tsubushi-an_ partially mashed); *the foundational confectionery filling of Japan*, used in dorayaki, manju, mochi, daifuku, taiyaki, monaka, anpan, and most traditional wagashi.
- *Oshiruko / Zenzai (お汁粉 / ぜんざい)* — sweet adzuki soup with rice cakes; warming winter food.
- *Setsubun and traditional folk uses* — adzuki has folk-medicinal status for digestion and gentle diuresis, paralleling the Chinese formal-medical reading.
- *Macrobiotic tradition:* adzuki is one of the canonical macrobiotic beans (smaller, easier to digest than larger pulses); used in adzuki-squash-kombu preparations as a kidney-supportive food.#footnote[Japanese cultural and culinary tradition: standard Japanese culinary and folk-medical literature; macrobiotic reception in Ohsawa and Kushi.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_No classical Sanskrit entry; reasoned cross-system placement only._

*Reasoned rasa:* Madhura (sweet), Kaṣāya (astringent).
*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu, Rūkṣa.
*Reasoned vīrya:* Śīta (cooling — gentler than mung).
*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (pungent).
*Reasoned doṣa:* Pacifies Pitta and Kapha; Vāta-aggravating in excess (less than mung, due to slightly more snigdha quality from the higher fat).
*Reasoned karma:* _mūtrala_ (the strong diuretic — cross-system convergence with 利水), _grāhī_, _bṛṃhaṇa_ (light), _rakta-prasādana_; useful in śotha (edema), prameha, pāṇḍu.

=== Viruddha (modern)

- Generally well-tolerated.
- Oligosaccharide flatulence (cook with kombu in the Japanese / macrobiotic style; or with asafoetida in the Indian style).
- *Aflatoxin in poorly-stored adzuki* — flagged in some Asian food-safety surveys.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Adzuki bean is *not classical in Tibb*. The species is exclusively East Asian (Chinese-Japanese-Korean cultivation), outside the medieval Arabic-Persian medical world, and does not appear in Avicenna, Ibn al-Bayṭār, or the South-Asian Unani canon. There is no canonical Arabic name. Reasoned modern placement on the Tibb pulse axis would parallel the *māsh* (mung bean, see mung-dhal.typ) treatment — Cold and Dry pulse class, mildly nourishing and binding, suitable for convalescent fever-recovery soups — but no medieval source attests it. Modern Arabic-and-Persian descriptive name: _fāṣūliyā ḥamrā al-ṣaghīrah_ (فاصوليا حمراء صغيرة, "small red bean"). No substantive Tibb tradition.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *麻黃連翹赤小豆湯 (Mahuang Lianqiao Chixiaodou Tang)* — _Shanghan Lun_ formula for damp-heat jaundice with skin pattern; 麻黃 + 連翹 + 赤小豆 + 杏仁 + 大棗 + 桑白皮 + 生薑 + 甘草.
- *赤小豆當歸散 (Chixiaodou Danggui San)* — _Jīnguì Yàolüè_ formula for damp-heat anal abscess / bleeding hemorrhoids.
- *赤小豆湯 (Chixiaodou Tang)* — simple adzuki-bean decoction for edema and damp-heat conditions.
- *Topical pastes* — for skin abscesses, mastitis.
- *赤小豆薏米粥 (chixiaodou yiyimi zhou)* — adzuki + coix-seed congee; classical dietetic preparation for damp-heat.
- *Dose:* 15–30 g in decoction.

=== Japanese tradition

